.TH CYAML_SERIALIZE 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_serialize \- serialize a DOM tree to block-style YAML
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

char *cyaml_serialize(cyaml node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Produce a human-readable, block-style YAML document from the DOM
subtree that node names.  The function writes a scalar as plain text
when that is safe, that is, when the scalar makes a round trip with no
implicit doubt about its type, and writes it double-quoted in every
other case.  It also writes a scalar double-quoted when the first
character of that scalar is U+FEFF, because at offset 0 of a document
those bytes are a byte order mark and not content, and the parser
removes them.  A scalar goes out plain only when a YAML 1.1 reader such
as PyYAML or libyaml also reads it as the same string.  A string that
holds a tab, a '?', or one of the YAML 1.1 line breaks NEL (U+0085), LS
(U+2028) and PS (U+2029), and the string "=", therefore go out
double-quoted, and the writer spells those three line breaks as their
\\N, \\L and \\P escapes, which every reader takes as the same code
point.  A string that holds a C1 control character (U+0080 to U+009F,
other than NEL) or one of the noncharacters U+FFFE and U+FFFF also goes
out double-quoted, because YAML 1.2 does not allow those code points
raw and libyaml and PyYAML refuse a document that holds one; the writer
spells a C1 control as \\xNN and the two noncharacters as \\uFFFE and
\\uFFFF.  The same rules apply to a dictionary key.  A list uses the
"- value" block notation, a dictionary uses the "key: value" block
notation, and a nested structure gets two more spaces of indentation
for each level.

The members of a dictionary go out in insertion order, which is the
order of the parsed text or the order of the cyaml_dictionary_set(3)
and cyaml_set(3) calls that added each key.  Replacing the value of a
key keeps its place, and a removed key leaves the order, so a parse
followed by a serialize keeps the key order of the source document,
the same in every run.  cyaml_dictionary_first(3) walks the same order.
The element order of a list is always exactly the order in which the
caller pushed the elements or the parser read them.

A float goes out with the shortest of 15, 16 or 17 significant digits
that reads back as the same double, and its mantissa always carries a
\&'.': "1.0", "0.25", "1.0e+20", "1.0e-05".  Without the '.', "1" reads
back as an integer, and "1e+20" reads back as a string under YAML 1.1,
whose float grammar requires the '.'.  ".inf", "-.inf" and ".nan" keep
their spelling.

A dictionary key that is exactly the canonical text of an integer or
a float key, such as "16", "3.1" or ".inf", goes out with no quotes, so
it keeps its numeric type and a parse of the output canonicalizes it
back to the same key (see cyaml_parse(3)).  A key that a parse would
read as another type or as another text, such as "010", "3.10" or
"true", goes out double-quoted.

A key whose rendered text is longer than 1024 bytes goes out in the
explicit form, with "? key" on one line and ": value" at the same
indentation on the next, because YAML 1.2 limits an implicit key to
1024 characters and PyYAML and libyaml refuse a longer one.
cyaml_parse(3) reads the explicit form back as the same entry.

A float always uses '.' as its decimal point, whatever LC_NUMERIC the
process or the calling thread uses.  For the length of the call, the
calling thread runs in the "C" locale through uselocale(3), which
changes no other thread, and the call restores the locale of the
thread before it returns.

The function writes a custom tag of a node (see cyaml_node_tag(3)) as a
leading "!<...>" verbatim tag.  It never writes a core-schema tag
(CYAML_TAG_NULL, _BOOL, _INT, _FLOAT, _STR, _SEQ or _MAP), because a
node only ever carries the core-schema tag of its own type (see
cyaml_node_set_tag(3)), and the quoting and formatting rules around the
value already make sure that the value makes a round trip to that same
type without the tag.

The text of a tag always survives the round trip in full.  The function
writes as a percent escape every byte that the URI characters of YAML
1.2 section 5.6 do not allow literally inside "!<...>": the '%' that
starts an escape, a '>', a line break, a space, a quote, a brace, every
control byte, and every byte of a UTF-8 sequence, among others.  A '#'
also goes out as %23, because although YAML 1.2 allows it literally,
libyaml and PyYAML refuse a verbatim tag that holds one.  Every
conforming YAML reader therefore accepts the tag, and a second parse of
the output document gives the identical tag string.  This holds both
for a tag that came percent-escaped in the source document and for a
tag that cyaml_node_set_tag(3) attached, because that function accepts
any string.

The library allocates the returned buffer with the allocator that the
root node stores.  Free that buffer with cyaml_serialize_free(), or
with cyaml_serialize_free_mp() instead when the tree used a custom
allocator.

node need not come from cyaml_parse*().  A tree that the caller builds
directly with cyaml_create_list_mp(3) and cyaml_dictionary_set(3) obeys
the same 500-level nesting limit that cyaml_parse*(3) applies at parse
time.  This function refuses a tree that is nested deeper than the
limit and returns NULL for it.  Because it walks the tree with an
explicit worklist instead of by recursion, the call stack that it needs
is a constant that does not grow with the nesting depth: the limit
bounds the work of one call, not the stack that the caller must give.

A NULL node serializes in the same way as a CYAML_NULL node ("~\en").
This is a successful, non-NULL return, not a signal of failure, which
is where cyaml_clone(3) differs: it returns NULL for a NULL node.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A null-terminated YAML string on the heap on success, or NULL when the
function runs out of memory or when node is nested more than 500 levels
deep.  The caller cannot tell the two causes apart, because this
function has no separate error-string channel.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_serialize_flow (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_stream (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free (3),
.BR cyaml_serialize_free_mp (3),
.BR cyaml_node_tag (3)
